<section class="page-head">
  <p class="kicker">Limb III</p>
  <h1>Sacred Geometry</h1>
  <p class="lede muted">Where the pattern is real, where the mathematics is beautiful, and where the claims quietly stop being true.</p>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p>Geometry earned the adjective <em>sacred</em> honestly. For most of recorded history it was the one
    place where a human being could encounter something that was certainly true, could not have been otherwise,
    and had evidently not been invented by anybody. That is as close to a religious experience as reasoning gets,
    and the Pythagoreans, Plato, the cathedral masons and the Islamic tilers all responded to it the same way —
    by treating form as a doorway rather than a decoration.</p>
    <p>This order keeps that reverence and adds a ledger. Some of what circulates under the name is exact,
    provable and genuinely astonishing. Some of it is measurement-shopping: pick your lines, ignore the misses,
    publish the hit. We draw the distinction on every plate below, because a tradition that cannot survive a
    tape measure does not need defending — it needs editing.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

