<section class="wrap">
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame reveal">
      <h2>What Survives the Tape Measure</h2>
      <ul class="biblio">
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> The five regular solids, and the proof that there are only five.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> Hexagonal close-packing in foams, honeycomb, basalt and graphene.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> Phyllotaxis and the golden angle, reproduced in non-living systems.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> Minimal surfaces — soap films solving an optimisation problem instantly.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> Logarithmic spirals in shells and galaxies, from simple growth rules.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> The 17 wallpaper groups: every repeating plane pattern that can exist. The Alhambra's tilers found most of them centuries before the proof.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="frame reveal">
      <h2>What Does Not</h2>
      <ul class="biblio">
        <li><span class="myth">◆</span> φ in the Great Pyramid and the Parthenon — selective measurement.</li>
        <li><span class="myth">◆</span> Geometry as a carrier wave for healing, intention or water memory.</li>
        <li><span class="myth">◆</span> "Sacred" frequencies derived by numerology from shape.</li>
        <li><span class="myth">◆</span> Metatron's Cube as a blueprint from which the solids are "derived" — they can be projected into it, which is a different and weaker claim.</li>
        <li><span class="myth">◆</span> Any argument whose final step is "and therefore consciousness".</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="muted small mt-1">None of these are shameful. They are myth, and myth is a legitimate shelf in
      this library. They simply are not measurements, and must not be sold as any.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <h2>Draw It Yourself</h2>
    <p>Reading about this is almost useless; the knowledge is in the hand. You need a compass, a straightedge
    and an hour.</p>
    <details class="scroll" open><summary>1. The vesica, from nothing</summary>
      <p>Draw a circle. Put the compass point anywhere on its rim and draw a second circle of the same radius.
      The lens between them is the vesica. Join the two centres and the two crossing points: you have an
      equilateral triangle without having measured a single angle.</p></details>
    <details class="scroll"><summary>2. The seed of life</summary>
      <p>Keep the radius fixed. Walk the compass point around the first circle's rim, drawing a circle at each
      crossing. After six you are back where you started — proof, in your hand, that exactly six equal circles
      fit around one.</p></details>
    <details class="scroll"><summary>3. The golden section</summary>
      <p>Draw a square. From the midpoint of one side, swing an arc to the opposite corner and extend the base
      to meet it. The new rectangle is φ. No arithmetic required, and no mysticism either — just a right triangle
      with sides 1 and ½.</p></details>
    <details class="scroll"><summary>4. Then go and count</summary>
      <p>Find a pine cone, a sunflower, a pineapple. Count the spirals clockwise and anticlockwise. You will get
      consecutive Fibonacci numbers — 8 and 13, 21 and 34 — and the moment you verify that yourself, the
      difference between knowing and having been told becomes permanent.</p></details>
